{"qid":"Q837998","names":["Kingdom of East Anglia"],"title":"Kingdom of East Anglia","endonym":{"native":"Ēast Engla Rīce","lang":"ang"},"capsule":"The Kingdom of East Anglia (Ēast Engla Rīce) was one of the principal Anglo-Saxon kingdoms, occupying the region of modern Norfolk and Suffolk, founded by the Wuffinga dynasty in the mid-sixth century. Its most celebrated king, Rædwald (r. c. 599–624), was likely the figure commemorated in the Sutton Hoo ship burial and was acknowledged as bretwalda — overlord of the southern English — for a period. The kingdom accepted Christianity under Rædwald's successors but remained politically subordinate to Mercia through much of the seventh and eighth centuries. In 869 the Great Heathen Army defeated and killed King Edmund (later Saint Edmund the Martyr) at Hoxne or Thetford, absorbing East Anglia into the Danelaw by 870.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The kingdom's rough territorial extent — Norfolk and Suffolk — is well established; precise boundary lines with Mercia and Northumbria are not documented and are inferred from later administrative divisions.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of East Anglia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_East_Anglia"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Sutton Hoo","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sutton_Hoo"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Kingdom of East Anglia","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Wehha","nativeName":"Wehha","roman":"Wehha","start":534,"end":571,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Wuffa","nativeName":"Wuffa","roman":"Wuffa","start":571,"end":57